WEBVTT

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:18.690
<v Ira Glass>Jill's been a flight attendant for years, does long flights to Europe and South America, where they're on the plane for 10 hours or more. And it's such close quarters, and customers can be so stressed out and get testy, and she and her co-workers, of course, always have to seem calm and courteous. They need to totally control the cabin.
<v Ira Glass>吉尔当空乘已经很多年了，经常飞往欧洲和南美洲的长途航班，在飞机上一待就是10个小时甚至更久。机舱里空间狭小，乘客们往往压力很大，脾气也容易变得暴躁，而她和同事们自然必须时刻保持冷静和礼貌。他们需要完全掌控客舱。

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:24.340
<v Jill>And at the same time give off this illusion of being servants.
<v Jill>同时还要营造出一种服务者的假象。

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:24.730
<v Ira Glass>Right.
<v Ira Glass>没错。

00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:26.920
<v Jill>When in reality, we're in control.
<v Jill>但实际上，我们才是掌控全局的人。

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:34.320
<v Ira Glass>It's stressful, she says. And it's easy for them to get on each other's nerves, like with this flight attendant who was Jill's nemesis for a while.
<v Ira Glass>她说，这压力很大。而且他们很容易看彼此不顺眼，比如吉尔曾经有个死对头，也是一名空乘。

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:56.350
<v Jill>She just talked a lot. I think that's what bothered me a lot. So during our downtime, going into the galley, sort of getting away from people, it would just be incessant talking. And it drove me nuts. And I think my silence would drive her nuts. So we didn't get along.
<v Jill>她就是话特别多。我想这就是最让我心烦的地方。所以当我们休息、走进备餐间想避开人群时，她还是会不停地说话。这简直让我抓狂。我想我的沉默也会让她抓狂。所以我们合不来。

00:00:56.350 --> 00:01:02.310
<v Ira Glass>Now, of course, when flight attendants snipe at each other, if they're in the middle of the aisle with the beverage cart--
<v Ira Glass>当然，当空乘们互相讥讽时，如果正好处在过道中间推着餐车——

00:01:02.310 --> 00:01:15.510
<v Jill>We have to do it in a way that the passengers don't know what's happening. For example, she would say, "can you get me some more Cokes from the galley?" And I would say, "we're out of Cokes."
<v Jill>我们必须做得让乘客察觉不到。例如，她会说：“你能从备餐间再给我拿些可乐吗？”我会回答：“可乐卖完了。”

00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:31.960
<v Jill>And to the passengers next to us, that sounds completely normal. But she knows, and I know, that we're not out of Cokes. And she's stuck behind the beverage cart. She physically cannot get there and get them for herself. And so it is--
<v Jill>对于旁边的乘客来说，这听起来完全正常。但她知道，我也知道，可乐根本没卖完。而她被困在餐车后面，根本没法亲自去拿。所以这是——

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:32.720
<v Ira Glass>It's war.
<v Ira Glass>这就是战争。

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:52.560
<v Jill>It's war. It's this tiny, little symbol of hostility wrapped in this little sentence about Coca-Cola. She might try, like, "are you sure we're out of Cokes? Because I saw some back there." And I would say, "no, we're out." She'd be powerless.
<v Jill>这就是战争。这只是关于可口可乐的一句小话，却包裹着敌意的符号。她可能会试探说：“你确定可乐卖完了吗？因为我刚才看见后面还有。”我会说：“不，卖完了。”她就只能束手无策。

00:01:52.560 --> 00:02:05.220
<v Jill>If we have to do another beverage service, she might take all the red wine. And so I have to ask her every time for a bottle of red wine. And I have to ask her for something every time I need it. It's these little, tiny things that you would never know.
<v Jill>如果我们还要进行下一次饮料服务，她可能会拿走所有的红酒。所以我每次想拿红酒都得向她开口。我每次需要什么都得求她。就是这些你永远不会知道的琐碎小事。

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:22.620
<v Ira Glass>And then about 2, 2 and 1/2 hours into these overnight flights, Jill says, it'll be time for a sleep break. Some planes have special seats curtained off or hidden behind doors in special compartments, or up secret flights of stairs in certain planes, there are crew bunks above the overhead bins. Who knew?
<v Ira Glass>在这些夜航航班飞行大约2到2个半小时后，吉尔说，就到了轮流休息的时间。有些飞机会有专门用帘子隔开的座位，或者隐藏在特殊舱门后的空间，有些飞机的行李架上方甚至有秘密楼梯，通向机组人员的卧铺。谁能想到呢？

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:26.870
<v Jill>It's a very tiny, little space, literally above the overhead bins.
<v Jill>那是一个非常狭小的空间，真的就在行李架上方。

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:38.430
<v Ira Glass>So Jill would sometimes have to go to sleep with her enemy, with the nemesis. And she says that to function on the rest of the flight, they really do need to sleep, no kidding. And so they both know its truce.
<v Ira Glass>所以吉尔有时不得不和她的敌人，和她的死对头一起睡觉。她说，为了在接下来的航程中保持工作状态，他们确实需要休息，不开玩笑。所以他们都知道，这是休战。

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:56.560
<v Jill>There is no disturbing the other person's sleep at all. I learned to not toss and turn, not clear my throat, just be very still. I imagine myself like a vampire in a coffin, so that I'm not disturbing the other person, even if I hate them.
<v Jill>绝对不能打扰对方睡觉。我学会了不翻来覆去，不清嗓子，就静静地待着。我把自己想象成棺材里的吸血鬼，这样我就不会打扰到对方，哪怕我恨她。

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:10.250
<v Ira Glass>And then they lie there, side by side, at peace, hurdling through the air at over 500 miles an hour, each of them dreaming, presumably, very different dreams. Jill does not know what the nemesis would dream about, but she has certain dreams only when she's in the air.
<v Ira Glass>然后他们就躺在那里，并排安睡，以每小时超过500英里的速度穿过天空，每个人都在做着大概完全不同的梦。吉尔不知道那个死对头会梦见什么，但她自己只有在空中时才会做特定的梦。

00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:20.880
<v Jill>It's strange. I don't know if I should say this, but I do have crashing dreams on the plane. It's strange. I don't have those on the ground. I wonder if it's the turbulence.
<v Jill>很奇怪。我不知道该不该说，但在飞机上我确实会做坠机的梦。这很奇怪。我在地面上从不做这种梦。我怀疑是不是因为气流颠簸。

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:23.650
<v Ira Glass>What happens in the crashing dreams?
<v Ira Glass>坠机的梦里发生了什么？

00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:26.890
<v Jill>We just crash.
<v Jill>我们就是坠机了。

00:03:26.890 --> 00:03:33.570
<v Jill>[LAUGHING]
<v Jill>[笑声]

00:03:33.570 --> 00:03:57.970
<v Ira Glass>Well, today on our program, "Same Bed, Different Dreams," we have stories of enemies sleeping together or working together or just stuck together in some way with very different hopes and goals, stories of deception and star-crossed haters. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>好了，今天的节目《同床异梦》，我们有关于敌人在睡觉、工作或以某种方式被困在一起，却怀揣着截然不同的希望和目标的故事，有关于欺骗和受阻的怨敌的故事。这里是来自芝加哥WBEZ电台的《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。请继续收听。

00:03:57.970 --> 00:04:11.010
<v Ira Glass>Act One, "Dream Weevil." That title's going to make more sense as this story goes on. Just trust me on that. In 1986, a man and woman ran into a US embassy in Vienna and said they needed asylum.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，“织梦者”。随着故事的展开，这个标题会变得更有意义。相信我就好了。1986年，一男一女跑进维也纳的美国大使馆，请求政治庇护。

00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:31.380
<v Ira Glass>They were from South Korea. And they told embassy officials this incredible story of kidnapping and intrigue, and being torn apart and thrown back together, and years of dreaming about escape from their kidnapper, while, at the same time, convincing their kidnapper that what they really wanted to do was stay with him. Nancy Updike has the story.
<v Ira Glass>他们来自韩国。他们向大使馆官员讲述了一个关于绑架和阴谋、被强行拆散又被撮合在一起的惊人故事，讲述了他们多年来梦寐以求地想逃离绑架者，同时又要让绑架者相信，他们真正想做的是留在他身边。南希·厄普代克带来了这个故事。

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:42.050
<v Nancy Updike>The woman who was kidnapped was a famous South Korean actress named Choi Eun-Hee. I read a book about it by a writer and film producer named Paul Fischer. He spent years researching this story.
<v Nancy Updike>被绑架的女性是韩国著名女演员崔银姬。我读过保罗·费舍尔写的关于这件事的书，他是一位作家兼电影制片人。他花了好几年时间研究这个故事。

00:04:42.050 --> 00:04:45.430
<v Paul Fischer>She was compared at the time to Elizabeth Taylor.
<v Paul Fischer>当时她被比作伊丽莎白·泰勒。

00:04:45.430 --> 00:05:03.800
<v Nancy Updike>Elizabeth Taylor. Imagine if Elizabeth Taylor, past her prime but still famous from all her iconic roles, had just disappeared one day. Choi was that beautiful, that much of an icon in South Korea, and she was gone. She found out pretty quickly who had arranged this.
<v Nancy Updike>伊丽莎白·泰勒。想象一下，如果伊丽莎白·泰勒，虽然过了巅峰期但仍因标志性角色而家喻户晓，有一天突然消失了。崔在韩国就是那么美，影响力那么大，但她失踪了。她很快就发现是谁策划了这一切。

00:05:03.800 --> 00:05:26.740
<v Paul Fischer>When Choi was kidnapped in 1978, she was put on a boat, and the boat went to Namp'o Harbor in North Korea. And she actually has a photo, which is in the book, of her very first meeting with Kim Jong-il. He was there waiting for her off the boat with a photographer. And he had his photographer take a photo and then send it to her as a sort of--
<v Paul Fischer>1978年崔被绑架时，她被送上一艘船，船开往朝鲜南浦港。书里有她第一次见到金正日的照片，那是她下船时，对方带着摄影师在那里等着她。他让摄影师拍了张照，然后送给她，算是某种——

00:05:26.740 --> 00:05:28.030
<v Nancy Updike>You'll want to remember this.
<v Nancy Updike>你会想记住这一刻的。

00:05:28.030 --> 00:05:30.690
<v Paul Fischer>Exactly, remember that time.
<v Paul Fischer>没错，记住那个时刻。

00:05:30.690 --> 00:05:32.710
<v Nancy Updike>Remember that time I had you kidnapped and brought to me?
<v Nancy Updike>记住我把你绑架到我面前的那一刻？

00:05:32.710 --> 00:05:37.870
<v Paul Fischer>And it's a terrible picture. And he's beaming and smiley, and she's got her face covered.
<v Paul Fischer>那是一张糟糕的照片。他满面春风，而她遮住了脸。

00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:57.230
<v Nancy Updike>For years, her family and her country didn't know where she was. This happened in 1978. Choi was in her 50s. And her first sickening guess as to why she had been taken was that Kim Jong-il had brought her to North Korea to be a mistress for his father, the Great Leader, Kim Il-sung, who was ruling the country at the time.
<v Nancy Updike>多年来，她的家人和国家都不知道她在哪里。这件事发生在1978年。崔当时50多岁。她对绑架动机的第一个令人作呕的猜测是，金正日把她带到朝鲜是想让她做他父亲、时任最高领导人金日成的玩物。

00:05:57.230 --> 00:06:24.250
<v Nancy Updike>But no, it seemed that Kim Jong-il himself was just a very big fan of Choi's movies. He put her up in a fancy villa, surrounded by walls and barbed wire and armed guards. She called it in her memoir "Las Vegas meets Vladivostok." Kim Jong-il brought her out to display at parties. That was Choi's life for five years, until one particular night and one special party.
<v Nancy Updike>但并不是，看起来金正日本人只是崔电影的超级粉丝。他把她安顿在一栋围着高墙、铁丝网和武装警卫的豪华别墅里。她在回忆录中将其称为“拉斯维加斯遇见符拉迪沃斯托克”。金正日带她出席各种派对。这就是崔五年来的生活，直到某天晚上的一个特殊派对。

00:06:24.250 --> 00:06:29.610
<v Paul Fischer>Choi had been there for a while. And she was standing somewhere in the center of the room.
<v Paul Fischer>崔在那里待了一段时间。她当时正站在房间中央。

00:06:29.610 --> 00:06:31.570
<v Nancy Updike>And she was used to these parties by this point.
<v Nancy Updike>那时候她已经习惯了这种派对。

00:06:31.570 --> 00:06:50.860
<v Paul Fischer>Exactly. They were routine. She had started feeling repulsed by them. People got drunk and groped women, and then they went on forever. And somebody had already told her that this is going to be such a great evening for you. And she nodded along, because she was used to hyperbole about everything.
<v Paul Fischer>没错。这些都是家常便饭。她开始对这些聚会感到厌恶。人们喝得烂醉，猥亵女性，而且派对没完没了。已经有人告诉过她：“今晚对你来说会是一个很棒的夜晚。”她只是随口应付，因为她已经习惯了对一切事物的夸大其词。

00:06:50.860 --> 00:07:19.820
<v Nancy Updike>Choi had no idea that Kim Jong-il that night was planning to show off for the first time another person he had acquired, the man who had directed or produced almost all of Choi's movies, her ex-husband, Shin Sang-Ok. Shin and Choi each didn't know that the other was also in the country. Shin had been brought there the same way Choi was. But he tried to escape twice, so he was thrown in a prison camp for a couple of years.
<v Nancy Updike>崔不知道金正日那天晚上计划第一次炫耀他获得的另一个人，也就是几乎执导或制作了崔所有电影的前夫——申相玉。申和崔都不知道对方也在朝鲜。申是以和崔同样的方式被带到这里的。但他试图逃跑两次，所以被扔进监狱关了几年。

00:07:19.820 --> 00:07:42.320
<v Paul Fischer>The best way to describe Shin-- Steven Spielberg's a good comparison, in a sense, as well. He made films that were very good, very commercial, very popular. And he also produced other people's films, had his own studio. And he was the most powerful popular filmmaker in South Korea, arguably in all of Southeast Asia for most of the '60s and into the '70s.
<v Paul Fischer>描述申最好的方式是拿史蒂文·斯皮尔伯格做类比。他制作的电影非常好，非常商业化，也非常受欢迎。他还制作别人的电影，有自己的工作室。在60年代和70年代的大部分时间里，他是韩国、甚至可以说是整个东南亚最有权势的流行电影制作人。

00:07:42.320 --> 00:08:01.390
<v Paul Fischer>And Shin, 10 days prior to this, he was still in a prison camp, got released, and for 10 days in a villa, they fattened him up, tried to restore him to health. He arrives at the party when it's already in full flow. And he walks inside, and as soon as he enters there's applause.
<v Paul Fischer>就在此前的10天，申还在监狱里，随后被释放。在别墅里被关了10天，他们把他喂胖，试图恢复他的健康。当派对进行到最高潮时，他走了进来。他一走进去，现场就响起了掌声。

00:08:01.390 --> 00:08:08.150
<v Nancy Updike>What an insane thing. 10 days ago, you're in a prison camp. You walk in, and you're the life of the party. Everyone's applauding.
<v Nancy Updike>太荒唐了。10天前你还在监狱里。你一走进去，就成了派对的核心人物。每个人都在鼓掌。

00:08:08.150 --> 00:08:14.050
<v Paul Fischer>Exactly. And in the pictures, his face still looks emaciated. You can tell he's just gone through something terrible. The suit doesn't fit.
<v Paul Fischer>没错。照片里他的脸看起来仍然消瘦。你能看出来他刚经历过什么可怕的事情。西装也不合身。

00:08:14.050 --> 00:08:19.190
<v Nancy Updike>Now Choi doesn't see Shin come in. She just hears people start applauding.
<v Nancy Updike>崔并没有看见申走进来。她只是听见人们开始鼓掌。

00:08:19.190 --> 00:08:25.280
<v Paul Fischer>And Choi is sort of even unfazed by this. Any time Kim does anything, people get up and applaud.
<v Paul Fischer>崔对此甚至无动于衷。金正日做任何事，人们都会起立鼓掌。

00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:27.220
<v Nancy Updike>She didn't know why people were applauding. She had no idea.
<v Nancy Updike>她不知道人们为什么鼓掌。她完全不知情。

00:08:27.220 --> 00:08:42.220
<v Paul Fischer>Exactly, until somebody says, "why don't you look happy? Look who's here." And she turns around. And it's been five years. Shin's got the marks of having been tortured in a prison camp. She doesn't recognize him right away.
<v Paul Fischer>没错，直到有人说：“你怎么看起来不高兴？看看谁来了。”她转过身。已经五年了。申身上带着在监狱受刑的痕迹。她没有立刻认出他来。

00:08:42.220 --> 00:08:57.580
<v Paul Fischer>And from that point on, Kim Jong-il was pulling them together. And they, sort of shell shocked, hugged one another. And then were then moved around, like blocking in a movie scene, by Kim Jong-il.
<v Paul Fischer>从那一刻起，金正日就把他们凑在了一起。他们像是受到惊吓一样拥抱了彼此。随后又像是在拍电影场景调度一样，被金正日摆布着。

00:08:57.580 --> 00:08:59.410
<v Nancy Updike>What, like stand here, sit here.
<v Nancy Updike>什么意思，像是站在这里、坐在那里？

00:08:59.410 --> 00:09:12.640
<v Paul Fischer>Stand here. You stand next to me. Smile to the cameras. They were sort of the guests of honor.
<v Paul Fischer>站在这里。你站在我旁边。对摄像机微笑。他们就像是贵宾。

00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:45.310
<v Nancy Updike>Famous people don't get to pick their fans. Kim Jong-il was a super fan who was also, unfortunately, the son of the all powerful leader of a totalitarian state. Shin and Choi had made their best movies together. Kim loved their movies, and he wanted them to be a couple, just like they were in their heyday. So he arranged it, parent trap style. He told them at the party that, guess what, he had a newly remodeled villa for them that they would be living in from now on, together.
<v Nancy Updike>名人没法选择自己的粉丝。金正日是一个超级粉丝，同时不幸的是，他还是一个极权国家至高无上领导人的儿子。申和崔曾一起制作了他们最好的电影。金正日热爱他们的电影，他希望他们能像全盛时期那样重新成为一对。所以他像《天生一对》里那样安排了这一切。他在派对上告诉他们，猜怎么着，他给他们准备了一栋新装修的别墅，从今往后他们要住在一起。

00:09:45.310 --> 00:09:50.430
<v Paul Fischer>Making a joke, I think he said something like, the newlyweds are going to want to be alone now.
<v Paul Fischer>我想他开玩笑说了句类似“新婚夫妇现在肯定想独处了”的话。

00:09:50.430 --> 00:09:51.370
<v Nancy Updike>What a weirdo.
<v Nancy Updike>真是个怪人。

00:09:51.370 --> 00:10:06.590
<v Paul Fischer>And had them taken to a car, and by all accounts, seemed extremely pleased, and expected them to be equally so. And it's the moment right after that when they went back to the villa, closed the bathroom door, turned the faucets on--
<v Paul Fischer>他让人把他们带回了车里。据各方说法，他看起来非常满意，并期待他们也能同样满意。就在那之后他们回到别墅，关上浴室门，打开水龙头的那一刻——

00:10:06.590 --> 00:10:10.910
<v Nancy Updike>In order to talk privately, they turned the faucets on so they can whisper and actually have a private conversation.
<v Nancy Updike>为了私下交谈，他们打开水龙头，这样他们就可以耳语，进行真正的私下对话。

00:10:10.910 --> 00:10:30.060
<v Paul Fischer>Exactly. And they just asked one another what had happened. The way she describes it is sort of a lovely moment, where she asks him, what happened to you? And he said, a lot. I'll tell you some other time. And then he asks her, what happened to you? And she says, a lot. I'll tell you some other time.
<v Paul Fischer>没错。他们只是询问对方发生了什么。她描述的那种方式是一种可爱的时刻，她问他：你经历了什么？他说：很多。以后再告诉你。然后他问她：你经历了什么？她说：很多。以后再告诉你。

00:10:30.060 --> 00:10:57.950
<v Paul Fischer>And Shin kind of gingerly, again, asked her, you know, you looked very smiley and happy. And have you been brainwashed? And then she made a joke about how the director can't even recognize acting anymore. And both of them were finally able to be genuine with someone. And they spent the next several hours going through everything.
<v Paul Fischer>申小心翼翼地又问她：你看起来笑得很开心。你被洗脑了吗？然后她开了个玩笑，说导演连演戏都认不出来了。他们两人终于能够对某人展现真实自我。接下来的几个小时里，他们讲述了发生的一切。

00:10:57.950 --> 00:11:25.800
<v Nancy Updike>Shin and Choi made a long-term plan and a short-term plan. Long-term, they were going to escape. But they would wait until they could do it right so they wouldn't be caught. Their short-term plan involved a small tape recorder, which they were able to get, because one of the weird privileges of their captivity was that they were allowed to shop at a department store that sold things that were out of reach for most people in the country, like tape recorders.
<v Nancy Updike>申和崔制定了长期计划和短期计划。长期计划是逃跑。但他们会等待机会，直到能够完美执行而不被抓住。他们的短期计划涉及到一个小录音机，他们之所以能弄到这个，是因为他们被俘的怪异特权之一，就是允许在一家百货公司购物，那里出售大多数国家普通人无法触及的商品，比如录音机。

00:11:25.800 --> 00:11:38.780
<v Nancy Updike>Kim Jong-il had long- and short-term plans of his own for them. He told them that soon he would summon them to talk about his vision for their future. Then seven months went by.
<v Nancy Updike>金正日对他们也有长期和短期计划。他告诉他们，很快就会召见他们，谈谈他对他们未来的构想。然后七个月过去了。

00:11:38.780 --> 00:11:53.110
<v Nancy Updike>And during those months, Shin and Choi spent hours nearly every week in Kim's huge film archive. He wanted Shin and Choi to see it and be impressed, and they were. Kim didn't just loved their movies. He loved all movies.
<v Nancy Updike>在那几个月里，申和崔每周几乎都要花几个小时在金正日巨大的电影资料库里。他想让申和崔看到并留下深刻印象，他们确实被震撼了。金正日不仅热爱他们的电影。他热爱所有电影。

00:11:53.110 --> 00:12:22.830
<v Nancy Updike>It was against the law for ordinary North Koreans to watch foreign films. But Kim had set up a massive bootlegging operation running in countries all over the world so he could build up his private collection with every movie ever made, or as close as he could get. The archive was three stories high and heavily guarded, at least two screening rooms, all for Kim only, and now for Shin and Choi. Shin was bowled over by every detail.
<v Nancy Updike>对于普通朝鲜人来说，观看外国电影是违法的。但金正日建立了一个巨大的走私网络，在世界各国运作，以便他能建立起拥有每一部有史以来拍摄的电影的私人收藏，或者尽可能接近这个目标。这个资料库有三层楼高，守卫森严，至少有两个放映室，全部仅供金正日一人使用，现在又加上了申和崔。申被每一个细节震撼了。

00:12:22.830 --> 00:12:41.440
<v Paul Fischer>The way the building itself was set up to run, and with admiration over how Kim had set up his own archive, and how well everything was kept, geeking out over the air conditioning settings and how he was preserving the celluloid, and how everything was cataloged, and the staff, and then how large it was.
<v Paul Fischer>包括建筑本身的运作方式，以及对金正日建立自己资料库的方式的钦佩，对一切保持得如此完好的赞叹，对空调设置的钻研，他如何保存胶片，如何进行编目，员工配备，以及规模之大。

00:12:41.440 --> 00:12:45.450
<v Nancy Updike>How is it categorized? Was it by year or by genre or by director?
<v Nancy Updike>是怎么分类的？是按年份、流派还是导演？

00:12:45.450 --> 00:13:06.630
<v Paul Fischer>It went by country first. And then I think it went by year as they got them in. And then there's a lovely touch where the South Korean films are in a normal air conditioning room, and then the North Korean films were in the most lavish storage and screening room in the whole building.
<v Paul Fischer>首先是按国家分类。然后我想随着他们拿到电影，就是按年份分类。还有一个可爱的细节，南韩电影放在普通空调房间里，而北韩电影却存放在整栋建筑中最奢华的存储和放映室里。

00:13:06.630 --> 00:13:25.260
<v Nancy Updike>The films were dubbed into Korean after being painstakingly transcribed, sometimes by Americans. There was an American defector-- he was one of several, actually-- who had been there since 1965. And one of his jobs was transcribing American films, but just by listening, because he wasn't allowed to watch them.
<v Nancy Updike>这些电影经过辛苦的转录后被配成韩语，有时由美国人完成。有一位美国投诚者——实际上他是其中的一位——从1965年就在那里了。他的工作之一就是转录美国电影，但他只能通过听力，因为他不允许观看。

00:13:25.260 --> 00:13:36.240
<v Paul Fischer>Yeah. And he wouldn't know that they were films, because they gave him and the other American defectors the audio. For some reason they chopped up the film soundtracks into different bits in the wrong order so they wouldn't--
<v Paul Fischer>是啊。他甚至不知道那是电影，因为他们只给了他和其他美国投诚者音频。出于某种原因，他们把电影原声带剪辑成了错误的顺序，这样他们就不会——

00:13:36.240 --> 00:13:37.410
<v Nancy Updike>Know what they were doing?
<v Nancy Updike>知道自己在做什么？

00:13:37.410 --> 00:13:49.620
<v Paul Fischer>Exactly, or enjoy them. But he had this experience of transcribing, and then suddenly realizing, wait, this is Mary Poppins.
<v Paul Fischer>没错，或者享受它们。但他有那种转录的经历，突然意识到：等等，这是《欢乐满人间》。

00:13:49.620 --> 00:14:09.000
<v Nancy Updike>Shin and Choi spent dozens and dozens of hours watching one movie after another in the screening rooms at the film archive and in their villa, which was also outfitted to show movies. They did that for months, until finally they got word. Kim is ready to meet with you. They went, and they brought the tape recorder.
<v Nancy Updike>申和崔在资料库的放映室以及也装有电影放映设备的别墅里，花了无数个小时一部接一部地看电影。他们这样做了几个月，直到终于接到消息：金正日准备见你们了。他们去了，并带上了录音机。

00:14:09.000 --> 00:14:21.170
<v Paul Fischer>And the second they walked in, Choi had the tape recorder in her purse, and she flicked it on. And I think unexpectedly, Kim Jong-il just talked at them, mostly, for two hours.
<v Paul Fischer>他们一进去，崔就把录音机藏在手提包里并打开了。我想意想不到的是，金正日大部分时间只是对着他们说了两个小时的话。

00:14:21.170 --> 00:14:23.810
<v Kim Jong-il>[SPEAKING KOREAN]
<v Kim Jong-il>[韩语对话]

00:14:23.810 --> 00:14:35.800
<v Paul Fischer>Kim Jong-il spoke very, very fast. And his mind went all over the place. And he had a very intimidating waiter slash bodyguard who kept walking into to refill drinks and empty ashtrays.
<v Paul Fischer>金正日说话非常非常快。他的思维跳跃极大。还有一个非常令人生畏的侍者兼保镖，不停地走进来加酒、清理烟灰缸。

00:14:35.800 --> 00:14:52.550
<v Nancy Updike>Shin and Choi wanted to record Kim for two reasons. First, they knew they might not be able to escape. Maybe they would be killed, and their children and friends would just never know what had happened to them. A recording, if they could smuggle it out, would at least be proof of where they'd been.
<v Nancy Updike>申和崔想录下金正日的话有两个原因。首先，他们知道自己可能无法逃脱。也许他们会被杀，他们的孩子和朋友永远不会知道发生了什么。如果有录音，只要他们能把它偷运出去，至少能证明他们曾经在哪里。

00:14:52.550 --> 00:15:12.240
<v Nancy Updike>The second reason to record him was that they knew that Kim would insist, they say, publicly, that they had come to North Korea on their own, voluntarily. Shin and Choi were hoping that, in their private conversation, Kim might admit to having had them kidnapped and brought to him. And he almost, almost did, by making a joke.
<v Nancy Updike>录下他话的第二个原因是，他们知道金正日会坚持要求他们公开宣称自己是自愿来到朝鲜的。申和崔希望在他们的私下交谈中，金正日可能会开玩笑地承认是他绑架并把他们带到这里的。他差一点，差一点就说了，通过开玩笑的方式。

00:15:12.240 --> 00:15:25.640
<v Nancy Updike>In the first couple of minutes of their conversation, Kim talked about how he had brought Choi over first in order to entice Shin to North Korea. I say joke because Kim laughs as he says it. And then all three of them laugh.
<v Nancy Updike>在谈话的最初几分钟里，金正日谈到了他是如何先诱骗崔，以此引诱申来到朝鲜的。我说这是开玩笑，因为金正日说这话时笑了。然后他们三个人都笑了。

00:15:25.640 --> 00:15:29.030
<v Kim Jong-il>[SPEAKING KOREAN]
<v Kim Jong-il>[韩语对话]

00:15:29.030 --> 00:15:30.900
<v Kim Jong-il>[LAUGHING]
<v Kim Jong-il>[笑声]

00:15:30.900 --> 00:15:47.560
<v Nancy Updike>It's tyrant management 101. If he laughs, you laugh. The rest of what Kim said in that conversation-- well, he said a lot of things. But the main thing was, North Korean movies are bad, and I'm sick of it.
<v Nancy Updike>这是暴君管理101。如果他笑了，你就得笑。金正日在那次谈话中说的其余内容——好吧，他说了不少事情。但主要观点是：朝鲜电影很烂，我受够了。

00:15:47.560 --> 00:15:51.150
<v Kim Jong-il>[SPEAKING KOREAN]
<v Kim Jong-il>[韩语对话]

00:15:51.150 --> 00:16:02.640
<v Paul Fischer>They were making the same film over and over again, and that there were too long and too boring, and history lessons and too melodramatic. And he says there's too much crying. I didn't tell anybody to put in so much crying.
<v Paul Fischer>他们一直在拍同一部电影，电影太长太无聊，充满了说教和过度煽情。他说有太多的哭戏。我没叫谁放这么多哭戏。

00:16:02.640 --> 00:16:06.180
<v Kim Jong-il>[SPEAKING KOREAN]
<v Kim Jong-il>[韩语对话]

00:16:06.180 --> 00:16:23.560
<v Nancy Updike>Kim Jong-il told Shin and Choi that he knew he was the only person in the country who could get away with saying what he was saying. And he told them not to repeat any of it. He said, when it comes to movies, we are in kindergarten. And other countries, like South Korea, are in college.
<v Nancy Updike>金正日告诉申和崔，他知道自己是这个国家唯一一个可以畅所欲言的人。他告诉他们不要重复他的话。他说：在电影方面，我们还在上幼儿园。而其他国家，比如韩国，已经在上大学了。

00:16:23.560 --> 00:16:43.370
<v Nancy Updike>He said, if we don't catch up in the next 10 years, our movies are going to be the worst of the worst movies in the world. The problem, he said, just between us, is that people in North Korea had it too easy. They had things handed to them. They didn't have to hustle, like Shin and Choi did.
<v Nancy Updike>他说：如果我们未来10年赶不上，我们的电影将成为世界上最烂的电影。他私下对他们说，问题在于朝鲜人民过得太安逸了。他们习惯了坐享其成。他们不像申和崔那样必须努力拼搏。

00:16:43.370 --> 00:16:52.730
<v Paul Fischer>There's a point in the tape where he tells them that-- and I think I'm going to paraphrase a bit. But he says, there's no incentive in socialism to do any better.
<v Paul Fischer>录音里有一个点，他告诉他们——我想我会稍微意译一下。但他说道：在社会主义制度下，没有动力去做到更好。

00:16:52.730 --> 00:16:55.890
<v Kim Jong-il>[SPEAKING KOREAN]
<v Kim Jong-il>[韩语对话]

00:16:55.890 --> 00:17:02.990
<v Paul Fischer>He says, my screenwriters, they'll write something, they'll get their rations. The other guys make a film, they'll get their rations.
<v Paul Fischer>他说：我的编剧，他们写点东西就能拿到配给。其他人拍电影，也能拿到配给。

00:17:02.990 --> 00:17:08.580
<v Nancy Updike>So they get paid no matter what, as long as they just turn out the work.
<v Nancy Updike>所以无论如何他们都会拿到报酬，只要交差就行。

00:17:08.580 --> 00:17:20.860
<v Paul Fischer>Exactly, and follow orders. And he seemed very conscious that he needs someone who pushes himself more than that, who's used to having to do more than that to earn his keep.
<v Paul Fischer>没错，听命行事就好。他似乎非常清醒地意识到，他需要一个不仅限于此的人，一个习惯于付出更多努力来换取报酬的人。

00:17:20.860 --> 00:17:42.470
<v Nancy Updike>Kim Jong-il never mentioned another possible reason why North Korea's movies were wooden and unambitious, like fear. He, Kim Jong-il, had written the book, the only book in North Korea about the right way to make movies and think about movies. It's called On the Art of the Cinema . It's still in print.
<v Nancy Updike>金正日从未提及朝鲜电影呆板且缺乏野心的另一个可能原因，比如恐惧。他，金正日，写过关于拍摄电影和思考电影的正确方式的书，也就是朝鲜唯一的一本电影书。书名是《电影艺术论》。现在还在印刷。

00:17:42.470 --> 00:18:03.790
<v Nancy Updike>Everyone involved in North Korea's film industry in the 1980s had to have been aware of the potential consequences of doing something as unpatriotic or insulting as trying to realize their own independent artistic vision. Without Kim's explicit blessing, and his father's, it was better to be crappy than sorry.
<v Nancy Updike>80年代参与朝鲜电影业的每个人，一定都意识到了实现自己独立艺术愿景这种不爱国或侮辱行为的潜在后果。没有金正日及其父亲的明确恩准，烂一点总比后悔好。

00:18:03.790 --> 00:18:28.720
<v Nancy Updike>The conversation continued. Shin and Choi murmured that North Korea citizens were fortunate to have a comrade leader who understood films so well. Kim Jong-il agreed. With his permission, the three of them got into critiquing specific films, a North Korean one called The Star of Korea , and while they were at it, a Soviet film called Mother that had a dying scene they all agreed had been atrocious.
<v Nancy Updike>谈话继续。申和崔低声说朝鲜公民有幸拥有一位如此懂电影的同志领袖。金正日表示赞同。在他的允许下，三人开始批评特定电影，比如一部叫《朝鲜之星》的北韩电影，还有在谈话中顺带提到了苏联电影《母亲》，他们一致认为里面的死亡场景烂透了。

00:18:28.720 --> 00:18:33.050
<v Kim Jong-il>[SPEAKING KOREAN]
<v Kim Jong-il>[韩语对话]

00:18:33.050 --> 00:19:04.470
<v Nancy Updike>They were three movie lovers talking about what they loved. And Kim Jong-il's big plan for Shin and Choi was that he wanted them to make North Korean movies good, so that they could compete with films coming out of the West and win awards and be shown in theaters all over the world. He wanted Choi to act in the movies and Shin to direct them. Shin could have his own film company, just like he used to have. Shin wanted to call the company Shin Film, a very bold request in the circumstances.
<v Nancy Updike>他们是三个电影爱好者在谈论他们所热爱的东西。金正日对申和崔的宏大计划是，他想让他们拍出优秀的朝鲜电影，这样就能与西方电影竞争，赢得奖项，并在世界各地的影院放映。他想要崔在电影中表演，申负责执导。申可以拥有自己的电影公司，就像他过去那样。申想把公司命名为“申影业”，在这种情况下这是一个非常大胆的要求。

00:19:04.470 --> 00:19:30.060
<v Paul Fischer>Yeah, nothing in North Korea had been called anything other than The Worker's Paradise this or The Party this or the Supreme Leader this, or Kim Il-sung University. So it was a big deal. But it's also very telling of Shin that, even in this surreal situation, his first instinct is, OK, OK, OK, but can we give the company my name?
<v Paul Fischer>是啊，朝鲜没有任何东西不叫“天堂劳动者”什么的，或者“党”什么的，或者“最高领导人”什么的，或者是“金日成大学”。所以这绝对是一件大事。但这也是申的特质，即使在这种超现实的情况下，他的第一直觉依然是：好吧，好吧，但我能用我的名字命名公司吗？

00:19:30.060 --> 00:19:56.670
<v Nancy Updike>In film noir, someone is always using someone, and often people are using each other. And we don't find out until the end who gets away with what. Shin and Choi knew that their only chance of escape would be to convince Kim Jong-il to let them film and work in the West. Kim Jong-il knew that if he wanted good movies, he had to give Shin and Choi enough freedom to create them. So all three of them wanted to make great movies. But--
<v Nancy Updike>在黑色电影中，总有人在利用别人，而且往往是互相利用。我们直到最后才知道谁逃脱了什么。申和崔知道他们逃跑的唯一机会是说服金正日让他们在西方拍摄和工作。金正日知道如果他想要好电影，就必须给申和崔足够的创作自由。所以他们三个人都想拍出伟大的电影。但是——

00:19:56.670 --> 00:20:14.680
<v Paul Fischer>Same bed, different dreams. Kim Jong-il wanted the films for his own purposes. And they needed the films to get away from him. And it's almost like his ambition of making films that the world will respect completely overtook any sense of logic.
<v Paul Fischer>同床异梦。金正日为了自己的目的需要这些电影。而他们需要这些电影来逃离他。这简直就像他制作电影的野心，要拍出世界尊重的电影，完全压倒了任何逻辑。

00:20:14.680 --> 00:20:38.500
<v Paul Fischer>So the first film Shin and Choi shot had sequences that they shot in Eastern Europe, standing in for the Hague. And they showed Kim Jong-il the film. He said, this is fantastic. It's like a European film. It's the best thing we've ever made, allowed them to start showing it. And it never crossed his mind that they had done exactly what he'd forbidden his own filmmakers to do forever for a reason.
<v Paul Fischer>所以申和崔拍摄的第一部电影，有一些序列是在东欧拍摄的，假装是在海牙。他们向金正日展示了这部电影。他说：这太棒了。这就像欧洲电影。这是我们拍过最好的东西，并允许他们开始放映。他从未想过他们做了他一直禁止自己电影人做的事，而且是有原因的。

00:20:38.500 --> 00:20:41.480
<v Nancy Updike>They'd been forbidden to show the world outside North Korea.
<v Nancy Updike>他们被禁止向外界展示北韩。

00:20:41.480 --> 00:20:59.920
<v Paul Fischer>They showed people things that they hadn't been able to see almost in a lifetime for some people in North Korea by 1983, 1984. And the second that film started playing in cinemas in North Korea, the North Korean audiences didn't take in necessarily the higher production values.
<v Paul Fischer>他们向人们展示了在1983年、1984年以前，一些朝鲜人一辈子都看不到的东西。当那部电影开始在朝鲜影院上映时，朝鲜观众未必是吸收了更高的制作价值。

00:20:59.920 --> 00:21:11.220
<v Paul Fischer>All they could talk about was the first 10, 15 minutes shot on the streets of Prague where, OK, wait, the buildings look amazing. People are walking into businesses. They're all dressed differently. There's cars. What?
<v Paul Fischer>他们谈论的都是在布拉格街头拍摄的前10、15分钟，因为：等等，建筑看起来太惊人了。人们走进商店。他们的着装都不一样。还有汽车。什么情况？

00:21:11.220 --> 00:21:26.220
<v Paul Fischer>That's what really had an impact, because up until then they had been told, you are the luckiest people in the world. The Worker's Paradise is the only place in the world where people have peace and freedom. And suddenly they saw that that wasn't true.
<v Paul Fischer>这就是真正产生影响的原因，因为在此之前他们一直被告知：你们是世界上最幸运的人。“工人天堂”是世界上唯一让人们拥有和平和自由的地方。突然间他们看到那并不是真的。

00:21:26.220 --> 00:21:28.310
<v Nancy Updike>How do you know that that's what people thought?
<v Nancy Updike>你是怎么知道那是人们的想法的？

00:21:28.310 --> 00:22:05.180
<v Paul Fischer>I was lucky enough to meet and speak to people who'd escaped from North Korea who were at varying ages at the time that they were able to watch these films. And there's another German researcher in Japan now called Johannes Schonherr who's done quite a lot of talking to defectors about the same thing. And one of the things that's fascinating is that Shin and Choi's films had a huge impact straightaway. There would have been people in their late 30s who'd never seen anything of the outside world that hadn't been filtered.
<v Paul Fischer>我有幸见到了那些从朝鲜逃出来的人，他们当时正处于不同年龄段，能够观看这些电影。现在在日本还有另一位德国研究员叫约翰内斯·肖恩赫尔，他做了很多与逃北者关于同一件事的访谈。令人着迷的一点是，申和崔的电影立即产生了巨大影响。当时会有一些30多岁的人，从未见过任何未经筛选的外部世界影像。

00:22:05.180 --> 00:22:38.200
<v Paul Fischer>But it wasn't just that people suddenly were able to see footage of the outside world. But Shin and Choi made a film called Love, Love, My Love, which was literally the first love story in North Korean culture post 1945 that wasn't about love of the leader or love the party. It was literally the first time people were able to go to the cinema, and see something that was about two people falling in love. And it was the first time teenagers had pictures on their wall of a matinee idol, of anybody other than the leaders.
<v Paul Fischer>但不仅仅是人们突然能够看到外部世界的影像。申和崔制作了一部名为《爱情，爱情，我的爱情》的电影，这字面上是1945年后朝鲜文化中第一部不关乎爱领袖或爱党的爱情故事。这绝对是人们第一次能够走进影院，看到关于两个人坠入爱河的故事。这也是青少年第一次在墙上贴偶像海报，而不是领导人的照片。

00:22:38.200 --> 00:23:04.410
<v Nancy Updike>Shin directed seven films for Kim Jong-il. And then in March 1986, he and Choi went to a hotel in Vienna for some film business, managed to grab a separate taxi from their North Korean minders, sped to the US Embassy, and ran in. They were debriefed by the CIA, and later by South Korean intelligence. And they were interviewed by The Washington Post and other media outlets.
<v Nancy Updike>申为金正日执导了七部电影。然后在1986年3月，他和崔为了电影业务去了维也纳的一家酒店，设法避开了他们的北韩监护人，坐上另一辆出租车，疾驰到美国大使馆，跑了进去。他们接受了中央情报局的简报，后来又接受了韩国情报部门的采访。他们还接受了《华盛顿邮报》和其他媒体的采访。

00:23:04.410 --> 00:23:20.840
<v Nancy Updike>And stories about North Korea are always hard to fact check. For parts of Shin and Choi's story, their recollections, their interpretations are all there is. But the movies exist. The recording of Kim exists.
<v Nancy Updike>关于朝鲜的故事总是很难核实。对于申和崔故事的一部分，他们的回忆、他们的解读就是全部了。但电影存在，金正日的录音也存在。

00:23:20.840 --> 00:23:42.010
<v Nancy Updike>Kim Jong-il had told Shin and Choi in the recording that when people ask them why they were in North Korea, they should say freedom, for true artistic freedom. For Shin, there was some bitter truth to that, because South Korea, at the time Shin and Choi were kidnapped, had been a military dictatorship with very heavy-handed censors.
<v Nancy Updike>金正日在那段录音中告诉申和崔，当人们问他们为什么要来朝鲜时，他们应该说是为了自由，为了真正的艺术自由。对于申来说，这其中有一些苦涩的真相，因为在申和崔被绑架时，南韩是一个实行严厉审查制度的军事独裁国家。

00:23:42.010 --> 00:23:57.510
<v Nancy Updike>Shin's studio had been shut down by the South Korean government in 1975. His career was in ruins. In North Korea, Shin got to make films again. He wrote unabashedly in his autobiography how much he had loved making movies there.
<v Nancy Updike>申的电影公司在1975年被南韩政府关闭。他的职业生涯毁了。在朝鲜，申又可以拍电影了。他在自传中毫无顾忌地写下了他有多么热爱在那里拍电影。

00:23:57.510 --> 00:24:20.790
<v Paul Fischer>Shin always spoke of North Korea as the place he associated film freedom with and creative freedom with, because he could ask for anything and get it. In one episode, he asked for a model train to blow up for a scene, and he was given a real train packed with explosives on rails to blow up if he wanted to.
<v Paul Fischer>申总是把朝鲜形容为他将“电影自由”和“创作自由”联系在一起的地方，因为他可以提出任何要求并得到满足。在一次拍摄中，他要求一辆模型火车来爆炸以配合场景，结果他得到了一辆装满炸药的真火车，如果他想的话随时可以引爆。

00:24:20.790 --> 00:24:28.710
<v Paul Fischer>And if he asked for a wind machine, he was sent helicopters. And if he wanted fake snow, everybody was flown to a mountain. There was no expense spared.
<v Paul Fischer>如果他要鼓风机，他们就送来直升机。如果他想要人造雪，所有人都被空运到山上。不惜任何成本。

00:24:28.710 --> 00:24:49.410
<v Paul Fischer>Kim Jong-il felt like the larger than life film producer that you hear about. And he didn't have the downside that a film producer usually has, because a film producer usually has to say no every now and then. We don't have enough money. We don't have enough time.
<v Paul Fischer>金正日感觉就像你听到的那些超越生活现实的电影制片人。而且他没有电影制片人通常有的负面一面，因为电影制片人通常时不时得说“不”。我们钱不够。我们时间不够。

00:24:49.410 --> 00:25:10.230
<v Nancy Updike>Shin and Choi had remarried in North Korea at Kim Jong-il's suggestion. And it ended up being a real marriage. They were together until Shin died in 2006. They seemed to have been each other's big love in life, besides movies. Although Shin loved movies more than Choi did, maybe a lot more.
<v Nancy Updike>申和崔在金正日的建议下在朝鲜再婚了。这最终成为了一场真正的婚姻。他们一直在一起直到申在2006年去世。除了电影，他们似乎是彼此一生中的至爱。尽管申比崔更热爱电影，也许爱得多得多。

00:25:10.230 --> 00:25:31.230
<v Paul Fischer>In their shared memoir of their time in North Korea, it goes from his voice to her voice, back and forth. And kind of the second they start making films, her voice kind of disappears entirely. And it's all his from then on. And it's about escaping, but in equal measure it's about making the films, almost as if he's writing a memoir of his time in Hollywood or something.
<v Paul Fischer>在他们共同在朝鲜度过时光的回忆录中，叙述在两人声音之间来回切换。到了他们开始拍电影的时候，她的声音基本完全消失了。从那以后全是他的声音。书中关于逃跑，但同样也关于拍电影，几乎就像他在写自己在好莱坞度过的时光。

00:25:31.230 --> 00:25:42.260
<v Nancy Updike>Choi's 88 years old, living in South Korea. When Paul was researching his book, he interviewed her for it. And he asked her about Shin and the movies they made in North Korea.
<v Nancy Updike>崔88岁了，住在南韩。当保罗研究他的书时，他采访了她。他问起申以及他们在朝鲜拍摄的电影。

00:25:42.260 --> 00:25:53.160
<v Paul Fischer>And when I met her and spoke to her, you could sense the impatience that if she had been able to get out of there three years earlier and not made any of the films, she would have been very happy to do so.
<v Paul Fischer>当我见到她并与她交谈时，你可以感觉到那种焦躁，如果她能早三年离开那里，没有拍任何电影，她会非常高兴。

00:25:53.160 --> 00:25:54.020
<v Nancy Updike>And him not so much.
<v Nancy Updike>而他可能没那么高兴。

00:25:54.020 --> 00:25:55.980
<v Paul Fischer>And him not so much.
<v Paul Fischer>而他可能没那么高兴。

00:25:55.980 --> 00:26:09.270
<v Nancy Updike>Even great love can't protect you from the other person wanting what they want.
<v Nancy Updike>即使是伟大的爱情也无法保护你免受对方想要的东西所干扰。

00:26:09.270 --> 00:26:34.850
<v Ira Glass>Nancy Updike is one of the producers of our show. Paul Fischer's book about all this is called A Kim Jong-il Production. Coming up, people sharing beds-- actual beds-- with very different dreams. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>南希·厄普代克是我们节目的制作人之一。保罗·费舍尔关于这一切的书名为《金正日作品》。稍后，分享床铺——真正的床铺——却怀揣完全不同梦想的人。那是芝加哥公共广播电台的《美国生活》，节目继续。

00:26:34.850 --> 00:26:49.610
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today on our program, "Same Bed, Different Dreams." We have stories of people who are bound closely together. They are stuck with each other, but with very different hopes and goals. We have arrived at Act Two, of our program, Act Two, "Smell You Later."
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天的节目《同床异梦》。我们有那些紧密结合在一起的人们的故事。他们被困在一起，却有着截然不同的希望和目标。现在到了节目的第二幕，《稍后闻你》。

00:26:49.610 --> 00:27:05.690
<v Ira Glass>There are all kinds of work situations where people very literally share an actual bed so they can do their jobs. These are people who share a bed that they are not ever in at the same time. This happens in lots of places. It has different names-- shift beds, hot sheeting, hot bedding.
<v Ira Glass>在很多工作环境中，人们确实共享一张床，这样他们就能完成工作。这些人共享一张床，但他们从不在同一时间使用。这发生在很多地方。它有不同的名字——轮班床、热铺、热床。

00:27:05.690 --> 00:27:17.310
<v Ira Glass>Flight attendants do this. The flight attendant, Jill, who you heard at the top of the program, used to sleep in an apartment in New York with bunk beds that she shared with a bunch of airline people. Guys who work on oil rigs and oil fields do it too.
<v Ira Glass>空乘人员这样做。你在节目开头听到的空乘吉尔，曾经在纽约的一间公寓里睡双层床，和一群航空业的人共享。在石油钻井平台和油田工作的人也这样做。

00:27:17.310 --> 00:27:27.210
<v Man>Basically the only rules that we had was basically we had to swap out sheets every time that we're going to go to our shift. Swap out your sheets and put my sheets.
<v Man>基本上我们唯一的规则就是每次我们要去换班时，都必须换床单。换上你的床单，铺上我的床单。

00:27:27.210 --> 00:27:38.520
<v Ira Glass>This guy works in Carlsbad, New Mexico. When he and a friend first moved there two years ago to do fracking, to save money, they rented this small RV, just like 15 feet long, one bed in it.
<v Ira Glass>这个人在新墨西哥州卡尔斯巴德工作。两年前他和朋友刚搬到那里做水力压裂时，为了省钱，他们租了一辆小房车，只有15英尺长，里面只有一张床。

00:27:38.520 --> 00:27:43.390
<v Ira Glass>He worked nights. His friend worked days. They had a rule to always shower before getting in the bed.
<v Ira Glass>他上夜班。他朋友上白班。他们有一个规则：上床前必须洗澡。

00:27:43.390 --> 00:27:57.150
<v Man>The sheets, my friend's always been real picky on that. He always had the color blue. I always liked red sheets, something bright. So every time that I'd leave, he always had my red sheets on. Every time that I'd leave, I'd always put his blue sheets on.
<v Man>床单方面，我朋友一直很挑剔。他总喜欢蓝色。我一直喜欢红床单，颜色鲜艳的那种。所以我每次离开，他总是换上我的红床单。每次我离开，我也总是换上他的蓝床单。

00:27:57.150 --> 00:28:04.740
<v Man>And plus, we never got the same sheets, because we never wanted to be mixed up and stuff. That'd be pretty messed up. I'd be smelling him, and he'd be smelling me and stuff.
<v Man>而且我们从不混用床单，因为我们不想混在一起。那样会很糟糕。我会闻到他，他也会闻到我之类的。

00:28:04.740 --> 00:28:18.190
<v Ira Glass>Migrant workers also have these shift beds in some places in farms around the country. In New York, the main agricultural product is milk. There are 5,000 dairy farms in the state, and lots of them operate 24/7, so shift beds are common.
<v Ira Glass>移民工人在全国一些农场也有这种轮班床。在纽约，主要的农产品是牛奶。州内有5000个奶牛场，很多都24/7运作，所以轮班床很常见。

00:28:18.190 --> 00:28:22.610
<v Ira Glass>Stephanie Foo went to see the shift beds at one of those farms.
<v Ira Glass>斯蒂芬妮·傅去看了看那些农场的轮班床。

00:28:22.610 --> 00:28:35.110
<v Stephanie Foo>I didn't see any cows on the farm. And more surprising, I didn't smell them. Instead, there were just sweet smelling bales of hay in a field with three mobile homes on it. Mexican music was blasting out of the closest one.
<v Stephanie Foo>我在农场里没看到任何奶牛。更令人惊讶的是，我没闻到味儿。相反，田野里只有干草堆，散发着甜味，上面放着三辆移动住房。最靠近的那辆正播放着震耳欲聋的墨西哥音乐。

00:28:35.110 --> 00:28:44.300
<v Stephanie Foo>My interpreter, Antonia, and I knock on the door. And as soon as we step inside, the stench of manure is overpowering from a few mud-caked boots in the corner.
<v Stephanie Foo>我和翻译安东尼娅敲了敲门。我们一踏进去，角落里几双沾满泥巴的靴子散发出的粪便恶臭就令人窒息。

00:28:44.300 --> 00:28:58.120
<v Stephanie Foo>It's a two-bedroom trailer with holes in the floor and black mold growing in the cabinets, and clutter everywhere, roaches, and jars of Nescafe and sugar and cans of beans, Coronas. It's 7:00 PM, and we're there during the shift change.
<v Stephanie Foo>这是一辆两居室的拖车，地板上有洞，橱柜里长着黑霉，到处都是杂乱的东西、蟑螂，还有雀巢咖啡罐、糖、罐装豆子、科罗娜啤酒。现在是晚上7点，我们正处于换班时间。

00:28:58.120 --> 00:29:11.610
<v Stephanie Foo>There are four or five guys milling around, all Mexican, all undocumented. Some are just getting home, and others, like this one guy, Jose, are just making up for work. Jose's shy, but he smiles a lot. He shows Antonia and me where he sleeps.
<v Stephanie Foo>有四五个人在走动，都是墨西哥人，都没有证件。有些人刚下班回来，而其他人，比如那个叫何塞的家伙，正准备上班。何塞很害羞，但总是笑。他带着安东尼娅和我想看他睡哪儿。

00:29:11.610 --> 00:29:16.250
<v Stephanie Foo>Oh, wow. There's a bed in the bathroom.
<v Stephanie Foo>哦，天哪。浴缸里有一张床。

00:29:16.250 --> 00:29:21.880
<v Ira Glass>Jose lifts up the bed's mattress and a wooden plank underneath, knocking over a jar of change, and--
<v Ira Glass>何塞掀起床垫和下面的一块木板，撞翻了一罐硬币，然后——

00:29:21.880 --> 00:29:24.050
<v Stephanie Foo>Oh. Are you in the bathtub?
<v Stephanie Foo>哦。你是在浴缸里吗？

00:29:24.050 --> 00:29:26.530
<v Jose>Si. [SPEAKING SPANISH].
<v Jose>是的。[西班牙语]。

00:29:26.530 --> 00:29:41.810
<v Stephanie Foo>He joked that if he wanted to take a bath, he could just lift up his bed and get in. His bed is a wooden plank placed over the tub. There are a couple of pillows shoved over the faucet, so that the knobs don't turn and flood the tub while he's sleeping. It's happened before.
<v Stephanie Foo>他开玩笑说，如果想洗澡，他只需要掀开床就能进去。他的床是架在浴缸上的一块木板。有几个枕头塞在水龙头上，这样旋钮就不会转动导致浴缸溢水。以前发生过这种情况。

00:29:41.810 --> 00:29:56.890
<v Stephanie Foo>Including the bathtub bed, there are a total of seven beds in the two-bedroom mobile home. Sometimes up to 13 people live here and share the seven beds, sleeping in shifts. Jose explained how it works. No, there are no alarm clocks. No need for them.
<v Stephanie Foo>包括浴缸里的那张床，这间两居室的移动住房里一共有七张床。有时最多有13人住在这里，共享七张床，轮班睡觉。何塞解释了它是如何运作的。不，没有闹钟。不需要闹钟。

00:29:56.890 --> 00:30:02.780
<v Stephanie Foo>When you get off work, you find someone who has an opposite shift from you, and you wake them up so you can take their bad.
<v Stephanie Foo>下班时，你会找到一个和你轮班相反的人，把他叫醒，好让你睡他的床。

00:30:02.780 --> 00:30:06.560
<v Jose>[SPEAKING SPANISH].
<v Jose>[西班牙语]。

00:30:06.560 --> 00:30:12.920
<v Stephanie Foo>Jose says, OK, I get here at 4:00 AM, and my friend is sleeping. And I tap him on his blanket, and it goes like this.
<v Stephanie Foo>何塞说，好吧，我凌晨4点到这里，我朋友在睡觉。我敲他的毯子，大概是这样。

00:30:12.920 --> 00:30:13.480
<v Jose>[SPANISH].
<v Jose>[西班牙语]。

00:30:13.480 --> 00:30:14.130
<v Stephanie Foo>Who is it?
<v Stephanie Foo>是谁？

00:30:14.130 --> 00:30:15.160
<v Jose>[SPANISH].
<v Jose>[西班牙语]。

00:30:15.160 --> 00:30:16.280
<v Stephanie Foo>Me, Jose.
<v Stephanie Foo>是我，何塞。

00:30:16.280 --> 00:30:17.650
<v Jose>[SPANISH].
<v Jose>[西班牙语]。

00:30:17.650 --> 00:30:28.690
<v Stephanie Foo>It's 5:00. It's 5:00. It's 5:00. And they try to go back to sleep. Let me sleep. And I say, it's 5:00. It's 5:00. It's 5:00. And they say, OK, OK. It's 5:00.
<v Stephanie Foo>5点了。5点了。5点了。他们试着继续睡。让我睡吧。我说，5点了。5点了。5点了。他们说，好，好。5点了。

00:30:28.690 --> 00:30:36.120
<v Stephanie Foo>Then I get ready for bed. He wakes up and says, OK, see you later. I'll wake you later.
<v Stephanie Foo>然后我就准备睡觉了。他起来说，好吧，回头见。我晚点再叫你。

00:30:36.120 --> 00:30:43.500
<v Stephanie Foo>And does it feel strange and intimate? Or did it at first when you had to share a bed with somebody?
<v Stephanie Foo>这感觉奇怪且亲密吗？或者刚开始必须和别人共用一张床时是这样吗？

00:30:43.500 --> 00:30:45.860
<v Jose>[SPEAKING SPANISH].
<v Jose>[西班牙语]。

00:30:45.860 --> 00:30:57.340
<v Stephanie Foo>Jose says, yes, of course, yeah. You're used to coming to work. And suddenly someone else comes and sleeps in your bed, in your blankets. You should see. It's weird. You flip your pillow over each time.
<v Stephanie Foo>何塞说，是的，当然。你习惯来上班。突然另一个人来了，睡在你的床上，钻进你的被子里。你应该看看。这很奇怪。你每次都要翻个面。

00:30:57.340 --> 00:31:01.560
<v Jose>[SPEAKING SPANISH].
<v Jose>[西班牙语]。

00:31:01.560 --> 00:31:09.200
<v Stephanie Foo>But you get used to it, to everyone's smells. We become friends. You know that if he showers, it's fine, but if not, I understand.
<v Stephanie Foo>但你习惯了，习惯了所有人的味道。我们成了朋友。你知道如果他洗澡了，那就好，但如果没洗，我也理解。

00:31:09.200 --> 00:31:12.860
<v Jose>[SPEAKING SPANISH].
<v Jose>[西班牙语]。

00:31:12.860 --> 00:31:23.060
<v Stephanie Foo>Because sometimes you don't get back until 7:00 or 6:00, and there's no time to shower. I'm understanding of that, and he should be too. It's fine. I mean, you get used to it, really.
<v Stephanie Foo>因为有时你直到7点或6点才回来，根本没时间洗澡。我理解这一点，他应该也是。没关系。我的意思是，你真的会习惯的。

00:31:23.060 --> 00:31:27.500
<v Jose>[SPEAKING SPANISH].
<v Jose>[西班牙语]。

00:31:27.500 --> 00:31:42.860
<v Stephanie Foo>That's what everyone kept telling me, you get used to it-- to the hours, to the work, to everyone else rummaging around in your space. You get used to it.
<v Stephanie Foo>这就是每个人一直在告诉我的，你会习惯的——习惯时间，习惯工作，习惯周围空间里乱糟糟的一切。你会习惯的。

00:31:42.860 --> 00:32:00.560
<v Stephanie Foo>Jose and Antonio work seven days a week, 16 hours three days a week, eight hours the other four, no overtime. I talked to a guy who's worked closely with many farms across the state, and he described the pay and living conditions at the farm we were at as, quote, "slightly below average."
<v Stephanie Foo>何塞和安东尼奥一周工作七天，三天每天工作16小时，其余四天每天8小时，没有加班费。我和一个在州内许多农场密切合作的人谈过，他描述了我们所处农场的工资和生活条件，引用他的话说，“略低于平均水平”。

00:32:00.560 --> 00:32:11.170
<v Stephanie Foo>Jose says it's not that bad. He says other farms he's lived at have been worse. At one, the roof leaked onto the beds. And it was so crowded that he'd try to sleep standing up.
<v Stephanie Foo>何塞说没那么糟。他说他住过的其他农场更烂。有一个地方，屋顶漏水滴到床上。而且太挤了，他试着站着睡觉。

00:32:11.170 --> 00:32:28.240
<v Stephanie Foo>At another, he couldn't sleep, because he was afraid that a drunk, violent roommate would beat him up in the middle of the night. Here, they get along. If someone wants to bring his girlfriend over, the rest of them all cram into the other bedroom, even sleep together in one bed so that guy can have a room to himself.
<v Stephanie Foo>另一个地方，他睡不着，因为害怕喝醉了的暴力室友半夜会打他。在这里，他们相处得来。如果有人想带女朋友过来，其他人都会挤进另一个房间，甚至挤在一张床上，好让那个人能有自己的私人空间。

00:32:28.240 --> 00:32:44.240
<v Stephanie Foo>But mostly, they're not too concerned about privacy. As I squeezed passed piles of other people's toiletries and clothes and sheets, and condom wrappers all over the floor, I was reminded of what a luxury it is to ever feel like you have a space that's all yours.
<v Stephanie Foo>但他们大部分时间并不太关心隐私。当我挤过别人的洗漱用品、衣服、床单和满地的避孕套包装时，我想起了拥有一个完全属于自己的空间是多么奢侈的事。

00:32:44.240 --> 00:32:51.610
<v Stephanie Foo>Here, at any given moment, somebody might be in the next room bumping the Black Eyed Peas off YouTube and singing at the top of their lungs.
<v Stephanie Foo>在这里，随时随地，可能隔壁房间有人正在从YouTube上播放“黑眼豆豆”的歌，还在扯着嗓子大唱。

00:32:51.610 --> 00:32:57.120
<v Antonio>(SINGING) Here we go. Here we go. We gonna rock, rock, rock. Easy come. Easy go.
<v Antonio>(唱歌)我们走吧。我们走吧。我们要摇滚，摇滚，摇滚。容易来。容易走。

00:32:57.120 --> 00:33:02.320
<v Stephanie Foo>This is a guy named Antonio singing. He got really excited at this part, because he knew the days of the week in English.
<v Stephanie Foo>这个人叫安东尼奥。他对这一段特别兴奋，因为他知道一周中几天的英文表达。

00:33:02.320 --> 00:33:04.410
<v Antonio>[SPEAKING SPANISH].
<v Antonio>[西班牙语]。

00:33:04.410 --> 00:33:10.770
<v Antonio>(SINGING) Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, and Thursday, Friday--
<v Antonio>(唱歌)周一，周二，周三，周四，周五——

00:33:10.770 --> 00:33:21.070
<v Stephanie Foo>Well, most of the days. All this is bearable because it's temporary. They do this a few years, then move home.
<v Stephanie Foo>好吧，大部分日子。这一切都是可以忍受的，因为是暂时的。他们这样做几年，然后搬回家。

00:33:21.070 --> 00:33:34.580
<v Stephanie Foo>And Antonio tells me he makes more in one day here than he would in a whole week in Mexico, and sends it all back. One man says he actually owns his own farm in Mexico, with his own workers, and that he's working here to be able to expand it.
<v Stephanie Foo>安东尼奥告诉我，他在这里一天赚的比在墨西哥一周赚的还多，而且全都寄了回去。有一个人说他实际上在墨西哥拥有自己的农场，有自己的工人，他在这里工作是为了能扩大规模。

00:33:34.580 --> 00:33:41.570
<v Stephanie Foo>Many guys have built businesses. One is putting two kids through college. Jose says he bought a big house for his girlfriend in Mexico.
<v Stephanie Foo>很多人都建立了事业。有一个人正在供两个孩子读大学。何塞说他在墨西哥给女朋友买了一栋大房子。

00:33:41.570 --> 00:33:52.710
<v Stephanie Foo>Another man puts it best. He says, every head is its own world. I come for my dreams, and he comes for his.
<v Stephanie Foo>另一个人说得最好。他说，每个头脑都是一个世界。我为我的梦想而来，他为他的而来。

00:33:52.710 --> 00:34:02.290
<v Stephanie Foo>One of the things Antonio-- the guy who was singing-- dreams about is his bed, his future bed. He can picture exactly what his bedroom in Mexico will look like once he's finished building it.
<v Stephanie Foo>安东尼奥——那个唱歌的人——梦想的事情之一就是他的床，他未来的床。一旦他盖好了，他就能清楚地想象出他在墨西哥的卧室是什么样子。

00:34:02.290 --> 00:34:06.260
<v Antonio>[SPEAKING SPANISH].
<v Antonio>[西班牙语]。

00:34:06.260 --> 00:34:11.280
<v Stephanie Foo>With a sturdy red floor, that's a color I like. The bedrooms each have a bathroom.
<v Stephanie Foo>铺着结实的红色地板，那是我就喜欢的颜色。卧室里都有个卫生间。

00:34:11.280 --> 00:34:19.010
<v Antonio>[SPANISH] with internet, [SPANISH], Netflix.
<v Antonio>[西班牙语]有互联网，[西班牙语]，Netflix。

00:34:19.010 --> 00:34:21.870
<v Stephanie Foo>What size bed do you want? And is it firm, or is it soft?
<v Stephanie Foo>你想要多大的床？是硬的还是软的？

00:34:21.870 --> 00:34:25.470
<v Antonio>King size, no? [SPANISH].
<v Antonio>特大号，对吧？[西班牙语]。

00:34:25.470 --> 00:34:29.140
<v Stephanie Foo>King sized waterbed. It's the best, no?
<v Stephanie Foo>特大号水床。那是最好的，对吧？

00:34:29.140 --> 00:34:31.350
<v Antonio>[SPEAKING SPANISH].
<v Antonio>[西班牙语]。

00:34:31.350 --> 00:34:41.080
<v Stephanie Foo>That's why we're here. Antonio cracks another Corona. It's getting late for him. He has to get up in six hours for his shift.
<v Stephanie Foo>这就是我们在这里的原因。安东尼奥又开了一罐科罗拉啤酒。对他来说时间不早了。他还要在六小时后起来换班。

00:34:41.080 --> 00:35:00.830
<v Stephanie Foo>I shake his hand, but before I go, I notice that I don't smell the manure anymore. I haven't smelled it in a while, actually. The air seems cold and fresh. They're right. You really can get used to the smell.
<v Stephanie Foo>我和他握了握手，但在离开之前，我发现我不再闻到粪便的味道了。实际上，我已经有一会儿没闻到了。空气似乎变得清凉而清新。他们是对的。你确实可以习惯那种气味。

00:35:00.830 --> 00:35:09.120
<v Ira Glass>Stephanie Foo is one of the producers of our program.
<v Ira Glass>斯蒂芬妮·傅是我们节目的制作人之一。

00:35:09.120 --> 00:35:23.570
<v Ira Glass>Act Three, "The Haunted Becomes the Haunter." This next story is about two women who aren't sleeping in the same bed. They're kind of inhabiting the same body, in a way, with very different dreams. Miki Meek tells what happened.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，《被闹鬼者成了闹鬼者》。下一个故事是关于两个女人，她们没有同床，但以某种方式占据着同一个身体，怀揣着截然不同的梦想。米基·米克讲述了发生了什么。

00:35:23.570 --> 00:35:34.970
<v Miki Meek>Jessamyn Lovell didn't know she had a secret doppelganger until she got a phone call. Jessamyn is a photographer who teaches photography at the University of New Mexico in Albuquerque. And one morning, she was heading out the door for work.
<v Miki Meek>杰萨敏·洛弗尔不知道自己有个秘密的替身，直到接到一个电话。杰萨敏是一位摄影师，在新墨西哥大学阿尔伯克基分校教授摄影。那天早上，她正出门去上班。

00:35:34.970 --> 00:35:52.740
<v Jessamyn Lovell>And I get this call, and he identified himself as Inspector Crudo from the San Francisco Police Department Financial Crimes Unit. And he immediately asked me, "do you know a woman named Erin Colleen Hart?" And I said "no, and why?"
<v Jessamyn Lovell>我接到了这个电话，他自称是旧金山警察局金融犯罪组的克鲁多督察。他立即问我：“你认识一个叫艾琳·科琳·哈特的人吗？”我说：“不认识，为什么？”

00:35:52.740 --> 00:36:10.200
<v Jessamyn Lovell>And he said, "well, did you ever give her your identification, your New Mexico State driver's license, or any other identification?" And I said, "no, no. I don't know her." And "what is this about?" And I immediately thought of my wallet being stolen a year and a half prior at this gallery in San Francisco.
<v Jessamyn Lovell>他说：“好吧，你给过她你的证件吗，你的新墨西哥州驾照，或其他任何证件？”我说：“不，没有。我不认识她。”“这是怎么回事？”我立刻想起了我一年半前在旧金山那家画廊被盗的钱包。

00:36:10.200 --> 00:36:22.840
<v Miki Meek>She never got it back, and assumed it was gone for good. But now this police inspector was telling her that a woman named Erin Hart had just been busted using Jessamyn's driver's license, trying to check into a San Francisco hotel.
<v Miki Meek>她再也没拿回来，以为丢了就丢了。但现在这位警督告诉她，一个叫艾琳·哈特的人刚因冒用杰萨敏的驾照试图入住旧金山一家酒店而被捕。

00:36:22.840 --> 00:36:28.570
<v Jessamyn Lovell>Then he, said, look, I'll send you a police report. She's in police custody right now.
<v Jessamyn Lovell>然后他说，听着，我会发给你一份警察报告。她现在被关在警局。

00:36:28.570 --> 00:36:31.840
<v Miki Meek>And she was in police custody because she had been trying to use your ID?
<v Miki Meek>她被关起来是因为她试图用你的身份证？

00:36:31.840 --> 00:36:39.700
<v Jessamyn Lovell>Yeah, exactly. I don't know, I just kind of thought, that sucks that that happened, and that's that, and whatever.
<v Jessamyn Lovell>是的，没错。我不知道，我只是觉得，发生这种事真倒霉，事情就是这样，随便吧。

00:36:39.700 --> 00:37:01.070
<v Miki Meek>But then about a week after that, it became clear that Jessamyn's license had been used at more places than just the hotel. A collections agency demanded payment from Jessamyn for $500 worth of goods from a Whole Foods store. Unpaid parking tickets and toll evasions also started showing up in her mailbox. And then the rental car company, Hertz, began hounding her for thousands of dollars in charges.
<v Miki Meek>但就在那之后大约一周，杰萨敏的驾照不仅在酒店被使用了。一家催债机构要求杰萨敏支付500美元的全食超市商品费用。未付的停车罚单和逃避通行费也开始出现在她的信箱里。然后赫兹租车公司开始为数千美元的费用纠缠她。

00:37:01.070 --> 00:37:23.690
<v Jessamyn Lovell>I figured out and added it up, and it was all, like, three different cars that she had rented. So one she just returned. Another one she had clearly damaged. And another one, the boot had been put on it, and there were tickets, and whatever. I knew I had not rented these cars, and yet they would not believe me. [CHUCKLES] They would not believe me.
<v Jessamyn Lovell>我算了一下，这总共是三辆不同的车，她租的。一辆她刚退租。另一辆显然被她损坏了。还有一辆被锁了轮胎，还有罚单之类的。我知道我没有租过这些车，但他们不相信我。[轻笑]他们不相信我。

00:37:23.690 --> 00:37:37.400
<v Miki Meek>She was spending all her time on the phone and email, trying to tell them and other places that she'd been a victim of identity theft. And then Jessamyn got a summons to appear in court from Alameda County Court in Oakland. The charge against her was petty theft.
<v Miki Meek>她把所有时间都花在电话和邮件上，试图告诉他们和其他地方她是身份盗窃的受害者。然后杰萨敏收到了奥克兰阿拉米达县法院的传票。对她的指控是轻微盗窃。

00:37:37.400 --> 00:37:44.670
<v Jessamyn Lovell>So I was really freaked out. This must be connected to her, this woman, Erin Hart.
<v Jessamyn Lovell>我真的很害怕。这一定和她有关，那个女人，艾琳·哈特。

00:37:44.670 --> 00:37:52.650
<v Miki Meek>So she bought a plane ticket on a credit card and flew to Oakland. Once Jessamyn got there, she sat around in a courtroom all morning, waiting to be called up.
<v Miki Meek>于是她用信用卡买了一张机票飞往奥克兰。一旦杰萨敏到了那里，她整个上午都坐在法庭里，等着被传唤。

00:37:52.650 --> 00:37:58.170
<v Jessamyn Lovell>I was getting more and more ramped up. It felt like a lot of people were getting taken away.
<v Jessamyn Lovell>我越来越紧张。感觉就像很多人被带走了。

00:37:58.170 --> 00:38:00.450
<v Miki Meek>Until she was finally the last person in the room.
<v Miki Meek>直到她成了房间里最后一个人。

00:38:00.450 --> 00:38:13.660
<v Jessamyn Lovell>And then the judge asked me, "are you the one that sent this big pile of papers in to me?" And I was like, "yes. Those are all the documents." She's like, "OK. I'm going to look through them really quickly here."
<v Jessamyn Lovell>法官问我：“是你把这一大堆文件寄给我的吗？”我说：“是的。那些都是文件。”她说：“好吧。我这就快速看一遍。”

00:38:13.660 --> 00:38:15.150
<v Miki Meek>OK, what was in there again?
<v Miki Meek>好吧，里面又是什么？

00:38:15.150 --> 00:38:24.470
<v Jessamyn Lovell>All of the Hertz stuff, and then the police report, just to show, believe me, this isn't the only thing. There's all this other stuff going on.
<v Jessamyn Lovell>所有赫兹的东西，还有警察报告，只是为了证明，相信我，这不是唯一一件事。还有其他所有正在发生的事情。

00:38:24.470 --> 00:38:36.920
<v Miki Meek>The judge dismissed the case but told Jessamyn she should get the charge expunged so it wouldn't appear on background checks, which was going to be a whole other hassle. She walked out of the courtroom, and she was still completely worked up.
<v Miki Meek>法官驳回了此案，但告诉杰萨敏她应该申请撤销案底，这样就不会出现在背景调查中，那将是又一个麻烦。她走出法庭时，依然完全处于亢奋状态。

00:38:36.920 --> 00:38:42.380
<v Miki Meek>How much was this going to cost her? Who was this woman? And what else had she done posing as Jessamyn?
<v Miki Meek>这要花她多少钱？这个女人是谁？她还冒充杰萨敏做了什么？

00:38:42.380 --> 00:38:58.100
<v Jessamyn Lovell>I thought immediately, I'm going to get to the bottom of this. I'm going to figure this out. I want to know what it was she did. I want to know how she got my ID. I wanted to find her. I wanted to find her.
<v Jessamyn Lovell>我立刻想，我要把这件事查个水落石出。我要弄清楚这一点。我想知道她做了什么。我想知道她是怎么得到我的身份证的。我想找到她。我想找到她。

00:38:58.100 --> 00:39:10.880
<v Miki Meek>This is where Jessamyn's story changes from a garden variety identity theft case to something stranger. She had a photocopy of Erin Hart's driver's license in the file she took to court. So she pulled it out and looked at the home address listed on it.
<v Miki Meek>这就是杰萨敏的故事从普通的身份盗窃案转变为更奇怪事件的地方。她在提交给法院的文件中有一份艾琳·哈特驾照的复印件。所以她把它拿出来，看了看上面列出的家庭住址。

00:39:10.880 --> 00:39:17.790
<v Jessamyn Lovell>I had enough drive, just adrenaline, that I was like, I'm going there, just like, I'm doing this thing.
<v Jessamyn Lovell>我当时动力十足，全是肾上腺素，我觉得：我要去那儿，就像这样，我要做这件事。

00:39:17.790 --> 00:39:21.530
<v Miki Meek>And what was the thing that you were thinking you were going to do?
<v Miki Meek>你当时想做的事是什么？

00:39:21.530 --> 00:39:41.260
<v Jessamyn Lovell>I don't know. I mean, I seriously-- I wish I could tell you I had a plan and I had something I was going to say, and I just didn't. And I remember thinking, well, I want to park close enough that I can run and get into my car if I have to. [LAUGHS] I'm not even kidding, I really did think that.
<v Jessamyn Lovell>我不知道。我是说，我认真地——我真希望我能告诉你我有个计划，想好了要说什么，但我根本没有。我记得当时在想，嗯，我要把车停在足够近的地方，这样如果必须的话，我可以跑回去开车。[笑]我不是开玩笑，我真的那么想过。

00:39:41.260 --> 00:39:52.380
<v Jessamyn Lovell>It was this huge, huge, huge, huge, huge apartment building. And I got in. Someone went in, and I just snuck in after them. I'm going in. I know her apartment number. I'm going to go in.
<v Jessamyn Lovell>那是一栋巨大、巨大、巨大、巨大、巨大的公寓楼。我进去了。有人走了进去，我趁机溜了进去。我进去了。我知道她的公寓号码。我要进去了。

00:39:52.380 --> 00:40:07.510
<v Jessamyn Lovell>And so I'm walking through the halls, and I'd found the apartment. I just went up to it and knocked on it. And no one answered the door. And then I'm relieved. And I'm like, OK, well, I have to leave. And then I photographed the door, maybe one of the most boring pictures ever taken in my life.
<v Jessamyn Lovell>所以我走在走廊里，找到了那间公寓。我走上前敲了敲门。没人开门。然后我松了口气。我想：好吧，我得走了。然后我拍了那扇门的照片，可能是我这辈子拍过最无聊的照片之一。

00:40:07.510 --> 00:40:09.760
<v Miki Meek>Wait, why? Like why take a picture?
<v Miki Meek>等等，为什么？为什么要拍照？

00:40:09.760 --> 00:40:34.870
<v Jessamyn Lovell>I almost wanted just to have the evidence for myself that you went there, you did this. Almost like, I mean, I don't want to overstate it, but like a tourist's documents. I kind of felt like I needed to have this pin drop there. I know it sounds crazy in hindsight. It kind of does sound a little weird in hindsight.
<v Jessamyn Lovell>我几乎只是想保留证据证明我到过那里，我做了这件事。几乎就像是，我是说，我不夸大其词，就像游客的文件一样。我感觉我需要在这儿投下这一枚别针。回想起来这听起来很疯狂。回想起来确实显得有点古怪。

00:40:34.870 --> 00:40:52.250
<v Miki Meek>Jessamyn also went to the Hertz counter in San Francisco, where three cars were rented in her name, and the Whole Foods in Berkeley where she had a $500 debt, and Hotel Vitale. That's where Erin Hart got arrested trying to check in with her license. Walking in, Jessamyn saw that it was the type of hotel she'd never be able to stay at.
<v Miki Meek>杰萨敏还去了旧金山的赫兹柜台，在那里以她的名义租了三辆车，还去了伯克利的全食超市，她那里有500美元的债务，还有维塔利酒店。艾琳·哈特就是在那儿因为试图持杰萨敏的证件入住而被捕的。走进去时，杰萨敏发现那是她永远住不起的酒店。

00:40:52.250 --> 00:41:04.760
<v Jessamyn Lovell>And I was not prepared for how fancy this place was going to be. Right away, coming in the door, they had wine, or they had tea. And the hotel staff immediately asked me if they could help me.
<v Jessamyn Lovell>我没有准备好这个地方会这么奢华。一进门，他们就提供了红酒，或者茶。酒店员工立即问我是否需要帮助。

00:41:04.760 --> 00:41:24.040
<v Jessamyn Lovell>And as soon as I said this woman had tried to check in here with my ID, the guy knew. And actually, he said that usually when this happens-- and I was like, oh, this happens a lot? And he's like, yes. And when that happens, we ask them to leave, and they leave. And he said, this woman didn't leave.
<v Jessamyn Lovell>当我说这个女人曾试图用我的证件入住这里时，那个人知道。实际上他说，通常发生这种情况时——我就问，哦，这种事经常发生吗？他说：是的。当这种情况发生时，我们会请他们离开，他们就离开了。他说，这个女人没走。

00:41:24.040 --> 00:41:37.290
<v Miki Meek>The manager knew Erin was a fraud, because she got Jessamyn's name wrong when she checked in. She had a beautifully groomed Persian cat with her, and was standing in the lobby insisting that the license was hers. That's when the manager decided to call the police.
<v Miki Meek>经理知道艾琳是骗子，因为她入住时报错了杰萨敏的名字。她带了一只打理得很漂亮的波斯猫，站在大堂坚称驾照是她的。这时经理决定报警。

00:41:37.290 --> 00:41:53.100
<v Jessamyn Lovell>So they arrested her, and they took her away. And the cat was just left there with her stuff. And they were like, well, I guess we'll have to call animal humane. And then she came a couple days later-- she was released-- and asked for her cat at the hotel.
<v Jessamyn Lovell>所以他们逮捕了她，把她带走了。猫就和她的东西一起留在那儿。他们说，嗯，我想我们得打给动物人道协会。然后几天后她来了——她被释放了——在酒店寻找她的猫。

00:41:53.100 --> 00:42:11.170
<v Jessamyn Lovell>And they said, well, I'm sorry. We sent her to-- and she was really upset that the cat wasn't there. That definitely was the first moment where I had any kind of sympathy for her. And they gave her the number for animal humane. And then she left. Now when I went to animal humane.
<v Jessamyn Lovell>他们说，嗯，很抱歉。我们把它送到——她对猫不在那里感到非常难过。那绝对是我第一次对她产生任何同情心的时刻。他们给了她动物人道协会的号码。然后她走了。现在我去了动物人道协会。

00:42:11.170 --> 00:42:15.020
<v Miki Meek>Wait, you went to animal humane to also track down the cat?
<v Miki Meek>等等，你也是去动物人道协会追踪那只猫吗？

00:42:15.020 --> 00:42:25.960
<v Jessamyn Lovell>Well, to see if she had been there. And nobody knew anything about her or the cat. And so I just, OK, and I just left. And then I photographed the outside.
<v Jessamyn Lovell>嗯，去看看她有没有去过那里。没人知道关于她或猫的任何信息。所以我只是“哦”，然后我就离开了。接着我拍了外部的照片。

00:42:25.960 --> 00:42:39.540
<v Miki Meek>So Jessamyn goes home to New Mexico. But she just can't let this go. She's still dealing with the fallout, fighting with Hertz and a few other places over bills. And she decides to do a bunch of things that most of us would not do.
