00:51:43.850 --> 00:52:03.690
<v Jessamyn Lovell>I had shoplifted nice cheese, or I literally stole arugula, things that I thought and associated with class, middle to upper middle class. And I think that's what really was giving me this sympathy for Erin Hart was I could identify, in a way, with the desire to be someone else.
<v Jessamyn Lovell>我偷过好奶酪，或者字面上偷过芝麻菜，我认为与阶级、中产阶级到中上阶级相关的东西。我认为这才是真正让我对艾琳·哈特产生同情心的原因，因为我可以理解那种想要成为别人的渴望。

00:52:03.690 --> 00:52:20.460
<v Jessamyn Lovell>I could see how a path in my life could have led to my being in that moment. I really could. But it was, I guess, a little disappointing. I guess it was a little like, oh, I kind of wanted it to be like she was a criminal and did something bad to me.
<v Jessamyn Lovell>我能看出我的人生路径怎么可能导致我在那一刻。我真的能。但这，我猜，有点令人失望。我猜有点像，哦，我本想它是像她是个罪犯，对我做了什么坏事。

00:52:20.460 --> 00:52:42.890
<v Jessamyn Lovell>And it just wasn't that. None of that satisfaction of I got her. It was just this weird transition. And anger was starting to be sort of replaced with empathy.
<v Jessamyn Lovell>结果并不是那样。那种“我抓到她了”的满足感完全没有。这只是一个奇怪的过渡。愤怒开始被同情所取代。

00:52:42.890 --> 00:53:02.550
<v Miki Meek>What Jessamyn did next doesn't sound empathetic at all when you first hear about it. But for Jessamyn, it was the logical end of her search. She went through the hundreds of images she'd taken, and she put together a gallery exhibit. It had surveillance shots of Erin, the court summons, police reports, bills, and pictures of places where her ID had been used.
<v Miki Meek>当你第一次听到杰萨敏接下来做的事时，听起来一点也不显得同情。但对杰萨敏来说，这是她搜索的逻辑终点。她翻遍了拍下的数百张照片，整理成一个画廊展览。里面有监护摄像拍到的艾琳、传票、警察报告、账单，以及她身份证被使用的地点的照片。

00:53:02.550 --> 00:53:16.890
<v Miki Meek>She had done this once before with the surveillance photos she took of her dad. In fact, her wallet was stolen while she was setting up that exhibit, which is what sent her down the rabbit hole of trying to find Erin in the first place. She called the new show, "Dear Erin Hart."
<v Miki Meek>她曾为她爸爸的监控照片做过一次这样的事。实际上，她钱包被偷就是在她筹备那个展览时，那也是导致她钻进寻找艾琳这个兔子洞的原因。她把新展览命名为“亲爱的艾琳·哈特”。

00:53:16.890 --> 00:53:30.860
<v Miki Meek>And one of the items on display was a sealed letter in a Plexiglas box. She asked the people at the gallery to give it to Erin if she ever showed up there. It wasn't a dramatic letter, just a way of telling Erin that she'd been looking for her and wanted to talk.
<v Miki Meek>展品中有一件是一个有机玻璃盒里的一封封好的信。她要求画廊的人，如果艾琳出现，就把信交给她。那不是一封戏剧性的信，只是为了告诉艾琳，她一直在寻找她并想聊聊。

00:53:30.860 --> 00:53:36.390
<v Miki Meek>Jessamyn didn't see putting Erin's name and photos out in public as revenge. But not everyone saw it that way.
<v Miki Meek>杰萨敏并没有把将艾琳的名字和照片公开视为报复。但并不是每个人都那么看。

00:53:36.390 --> 00:53:41.730
<v News Reporter>If you were a victim of identity theft, would you consider tracking down the thief and turning the tables?
<v News Reporter>如果你是身份盗窃的受害者，你会考虑追踪窃贼并反戈一击吗？

00:53:41.730 --> 00:53:42.960
<v Miki Meek>This is from the Today Show .
<v Miki Meek>这是来自《今日秀》的节目。

00:53:42.960 --> 00:53:50.300
<v News Reporter>In an act some have described as revenge, Lovell followed and photographed the woman who stole her wallet, and then her identity.
<v News Reporter>在一些人描述为报复的行为中，洛弗尔追踪并拍摄了偷走她钱包、后来又盗用她身份的那个女人。

00:53:50.300 --> 00:54:17.200
<v Miki Meek>I reached out to Erin Hart through her probation officer to talk about all this. I sent a couple emails listing everything Jessamyn says about her. But Erin never got back to me. So I couldn't verify whether she was responsible for bills Jessamyn got from Hertz and Whole Foods, or any of the unpaid parking tickets and tolls, or whether she shoplifted from Goodwill or stole from her boyfriend.
<v Miki Meek>我通过她的缓刑监督官联系了艾琳·哈特，想谈谈这一切。我发了几封邮件，列出了杰萨敏关于她的一切说法。但艾琳从没回复我。所以我无法核实她是否要为杰萨敏从赫兹和全食超市收到的账单负责，或者任何未付的停车罚单和通行费，或者她是否在好邻居店顺手牵羊或从男朋友那里偷窃。

00:54:17.200 --> 00:54:40.360
<v Miki Meek>So Erin secretly posed as Jessamyn. And Jessamyn secretly photographed Erin. Jessamyn admits it's an uncomfortable thing that they have in common. Each of them is willing to invade someone else's privacy to get what they want.
<v Miki Meek>所以艾琳秘密冒充杰萨敏。杰萨敏秘密拍摄了艾琳。杰萨敏承认，她们共同拥有一种不舒服的事情。她们每个人都愿意侵犯他人的隐私来获得她们想要的东西。

00:54:40.360 --> 00:54:57.710
<v Ira Glass>Miki Meek is one of the producers of our show. We thought we would end today's program where we began, on an airplane. At some point in putting together this week's show, it occurred to us that an overnight flight really is just like this huge bed with hundreds of people all together, dreaming different dreams.
<v Ira Glass>米基·米克是我们节目的制作人之一。我们想我们以开始的地方结束今天的节目，在一架飞机上。在整理本周节目过程中的某个时刻，我们想到，一架夜航航班确实就像这张数百人共处、怀揣不同梦想的巨床。

00:54:57.710 --> 00:55:09.450
<v Ira Glass>And we asked people on two different flights like this to tell us their dreams, and their daydreams, and their hopes and dreams. One of our staff was actually on a red eye flight herself and got on the plane's PA system to explain the premise.
<v Ira Glass>我们请两架不同航班上的人讲述他们的梦想、白日梦，以及他们的希望和梦想。我们的一位员工实际上就在一架红眼航班上，并利用飞机的广播系统解释了这个前提。

00:55:09.450 --> 00:55:14.250
<v Stephanie Foo>May I have your attention, please, ladies and gentlemen? My name is Stephanie Foo.
<v Stephanie Foo>请注意，女士们先生们？我叫斯蒂芬妮·傅。

00:55:14.250 --> 00:55:17.390
<v Ira Glass>Somehow, being on the PA system, she started talking like a flight attendant.
<v Ira Glass>不知怎么的，通过广播系统，她开始像个空乘一样说话。

00:55:17.390 --> 00:55:21.040
<v Stephanie Foo>I am doing a story about this flight.
<v Stephanie Foo>我正在做关于这个航班的故事。

00:55:21.040 --> 00:55:25.640
<v Ira Glass>David Weinberg and Jessica Ament recorded people as well. Here are some of them.
<v Ira Glass>大卫·温伯格和杰西卡·阿门也记录了人们的情况。以下是其中一些。

00:55:25.640 --> 00:55:36.970
<v Man>I was dreaming about old fashioned TV antennas. And then at one point, I felt like I was shaking. And I was sure it was because the plane was shaking. But I was trying to adjust the antenna.
<v Man>我梦见老式电视天线。然后在某个时刻，我觉得自己在晃动。我很确定这是因为飞机在晃动。但我当时在试图调整天线。

00:55:36.970 --> 00:55:45.140
<v Woman>I was dreaming about I was in a hot air balloon. And I was yelling for my dog, hey, Katie, I'm up here.
<v Woman>我梦见我在一个热气球里。我对着我的狗大喊：嘿，凯蒂，我在上面。

00:55:45.140 --> 00:56:02.720
<v Man>Sitting there, semi-lucid, I was thinking of the horned puffins swimming underwater, and how the little bubbles come out of their feathers when they're swimming. And then I thought of them as going along in space and creating stars, and then diving into the center of the galaxy.
<v Man>坐在那里，半清醒状态下，我想着角海雀在水下游泳，以及它们游泳时羽毛中冒出的小气泡。然后我把它们想象成在太空中穿行并创造恒星，然后潜入银河系的中心。

00:56:02.720 --> 00:56:04.030
<v Stephanie Foo>That's really trippy.
<v Stephanie Foo>那真是太迷幻了。

00:56:04.030 --> 00:56:04.870
<v Man>Yeah.
<v Man>是的。

00:56:04.870 --> 00:56:06.100
<v Stephanie Foo>Were you medicated at all?
<v Stephanie Foo>你当时吃药了吗？

00:56:06.100 --> 00:56:10.580
<v Man>No, not at all. [LAUGHS]
<v Man>没有，完全没有。[笑]

00:56:10.580 --> 00:56:30.950
<v Woman>I had a total nightmare. I was backing down a hill out of a garage with a friend in a VW bus. And we lost control of it and went down the hill. And my friend turned to me and said, you know, I will never forgive you for this.
<v Woman>我做了一个彻头彻尾的噩梦。我正和一个朋友开着一辆大众面包车倒出车库。我们失去了控制，冲下了山坡。我朋友转过身对我说：你知道吗，我永远不会原谅你的。

00:56:30.950 --> 00:56:50.480
<v Woman>Everybody says, why would you want to go to New York? Ew, it's so big. And why? You know, and I'm like, because you see all these things on television. I've always wanted to experience a cab ride in New York City. We're going to do it all. We're going to do it all.
<v Woman>每个人都说，你为什么要那么想去纽约？噫，那么大。为什么？你知道，我说因为你在电视上看到了所有这些东西。我一直想体验一下在纽约市坐出租车。我们要全部体验。我们要全部体验。

00:56:50.480 --> 00:57:10.650
<v Man>My daughter's about to have a baby. So I don't know if dream's exactly the right word, but a lot of hopes. It's her first baby, my first grandbaby. Just to put it simply, I definitely didn't live up to my responsibility as her father.
<v Man>我女儿快生孩子了。所以我不知道“梦想”这个词是否完全准确，但有很多希望。这是她的第一个孩子，我的第一个孙子。简单地说，我绝对没尽到做父亲的责任。

00:57:10.650 --> 00:57:34.240
<v Man>And I had not been a regular part of her life up until she was nine. I blew it. So I'm definitely not going to make the same mistakes that I made with her. I feel like I've been given a second chance.
<v Man>在她九岁之前我一直没规律地参与她的生活。我搞砸了。所以我绝对不会犯和她当初一样的错误。我觉得我得到了第二次机会。

00:57:34.240 --> 00:57:42.420
<v Man>[MUSIC - "SAME BED, DIFFERENT DREAMS," BY JOHN HOWARD]
<v Man>[音乐 - “同床异梦”，约翰·霍华德]

